  <p>For an activewear buyer, sample approval is where an attractive concept becomes a controlled production reference. A sports bra, legging or matching yoga set can look correct in a product photo and still create bulk-order problems if the fit, stretch, opacity, stitching, labeling or packaging were never checked in a repeatable way.</p>

  <p>This <strong>activewear sample approval checklist</strong> is designed for brand owners, buying teams, gyms and e-commerce sellers preparing a private-label or OEM order. It helps turn subjective feedback—such as “the waistband feels tight” or “the logo looks too low”—into specific instructions a production team can verify.</p>

  <h2>What an approved activewear sample must prove</h2>
  <p>A sample should not only answer “Do we like this design?” It should prove that the chosen specification can be repeated. The final approval reference normally brings together the garment, the measurement sheet, the material decision, the color reference, the branding artwork and the packaging expectation.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Product intent:</strong> the silhouette, coverage and intended use match the collection plan.</li>
    <li><strong>Wearability:</strong> the garment remains comfortable and stable during the movements it is designed for.</li>
    <li><strong>Build consistency:</strong> seams, edges, inserts, hardware and trims are clear enough to reproduce.</li>
    <li><strong>Retail readiness:</strong> labels, logo treatment and packing details are approved alongside the garment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A practical activewear sample test sequence</h2>
  <h3>1. Check the specification before trying the garment on</h3>
  <p>Compare the sample with the latest tech pack, purchase notes or approved drawing. Confirm the style code, pieces included in the set, fabric composition, requested colorway and size. If the reference has changed, update the document before reviewing the garment; otherwise the review will be based on two different versions of the product.</p>

  <h3>2. Measure key points and note tolerance questions</h3>
  <p>Measure the points that drive fit: bust or underbust, waist, hip, rise, inseam, leg opening, garment length, strap length and waistband height as relevant to the style. A buyer does not need to invent a complicated grading system on the spot, but each important point needs a target and an agreed way to record deviations.</p>

  <h3>3. Test fit during real movement</h3>
  <p>For yoga leggings, test bending and squatting. For sports bras and tanks, test reaching, twisting and arm movement. For coordinated sets, check that the top and bottom stay balanced together. Record whether the waistband rolls, the neckline shifts, the inseam pulls, the fabric becomes too transparent or the recovery feels different after movement.</p>

  <h3>4. Inspect fabric, color and surface appearance</h3>
  <p>Review the fabric under normal retail lighting as well as the light likely to be used for product photography. Compare the agreed color reference, look for uneven dye appearance or marks, and discuss whether the hand feel matches the target product position. If you are selecting fabric weight, see our <a href="/blogs/fabric-weight-selection-guide/">fabric weight selection guide</a> for the questions to settle before bulk.</p>

  <h3>5. Review construction and comfort details</h3>
  <p>Look closely at seams, joining points, elastic attachment, coverstitch lines, pocket openings, hems, binding and hardware. Activewear construction needs to feel as considered as it looks: a seam that is neat on a table can still rub or restrict motion when worn.</p>

  <h3>6. Approve the complete private-label presentation</h3>
  <p>For a private-label activewear sample, inspect the garment and the commercial details together. Confirm logo location and size, care and size labels, hang tags, barcode requirements, folding, polybags and carton-marking expectations. A small detail missed here can delay a retail launch even if the garment is otherwise ready.</p>

  <h2>How to write feedback a manufacturer can use</h2>
  <p>Make every comment traceable. Use the same style code on the sample, file name and feedback sheet. Add photos with arrows or dimensions where possible, state the desired correction, and identify whether it is required for approval or a preference for a future colorway. Finish with one overall decision: approve, revise and resubmit, or hold pending more information.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Instead of saying</th><th>Use a production-ready comment</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>“The leggings feel small.”</td><td>“Size M waistband feels restrictive during squat; review the approved waist specification and recovery target, then confirm the revision.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td>“Move the logo a little.”</td><td>“Place logo 45 mm below the center front neckline, measured from the finished edge; confirm artwork scale before next sample.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td>“Color is not quite right.”</td><td>“Compare against the attached color reference in daylight; submit the next lab dip or color reference for approval before bulk dyeing.”</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Keep one final bulk-production reference</h2>
  <p>Once the sample is approved, archive it with the final comments, measurement sheet, fabric reference and branding files. The goal is not to create more paperwork; it is to give the buyer, factory and any inspection team the same answer when a question arises during bulk production. That is especially valuable when a collection has several colors, sizes or matching pieces.</p>

  <p>If your team is still defining fit, start with our <a href="/blogs/activewear-sizing-and-grading-guide/">activewear sizing and grading guide</a>. If the product direction is set but you are choosing a supplier, our <a href="/blogs/vetting-activewear-manufacturer/">manufacturer vetting guide</a> outlines questions that are useful before samples are ordered.</p>
